{"causes": ["The Sasanian Empire's inherent feudal structure and powerful regional nobility necessitated a strong central ruler to maintain cohesion.", "The growing influence of the Zoroastrian clergy, particularly figures like Kartir, provided an opportunity for monarchs to use religious unity as a tool for state consolidation.", "Ongoing geopolitical rivalry with the Roman Empire created constant pressure for Sasanian rulers to secure borders and project strength."], "effects": ["Bahram II's policies solidified Zoroastrianism as the dominant state religion of the Sasanian Empire, leading to increased persecution of other faiths.", "The rise of Kartir to unprecedented religious authority under Bahram II established a powerful precedent for the integration of clerical and royal power.", "Bahram II's suppression of internal rebellions contributed to the long-term stability of the Sasanian dynasty despite external Roman incursions."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "bahram-ii", "sourceName": "Bahram II", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "kartir", "targetName": "Kartir", "context": "Bahram II collaborated closely with the Zoroastrian high priest Kartir, empowering him to persecute religious minorities during his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "carus", "sourceName": "Carus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "bahram-ii", "targetName": "Bahram II", "context": "The Roman Emperor Carus's invasion of Mesopotamia in 283 CE caused significant military challenges for Bahram II, leading to the temporary fall of Ctesiphon."}, {"sourceSlug": "bahram-ii", "sourceName": "Bahram II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "sasanian-empire", "targetName": "Sasanian Empire", "context": "Bahram II's reign as King of Kings occurred entirely within the geographical and political boundaries of the Sasanian Empire (274-293 CE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "bahram-ii", "sourceName": "Bahram II", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-sasanian-wars", "targetName": "Roman-Sasanian Wars", "context": "Bahram II actively participated in the ongoing Roman-Sasanian Wars, notably defending against Emperor Carus's invasion in 283 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "bahram-ii", "sourceName": "Bahram II", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "bahram-i", "targetName": "Bahram I", "context": "Bahram II succeeded his father, Bahram I, as the Sasanian King of Kings in 274 CE, inheriting an empire facing internal and external pressures."}], "places": [{"name": "Ctesiphon, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Naqsh-e Rostam, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Royal Necropolis, Relief Site"}, {"name": "Mesopotamia, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Region of Conflict"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bahram II", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bahram-ii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q299255", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahram_II"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bahram II's reign significantly strengthened the Zoroastrian clergy's power, cementing its role as a state religion and influencing subsequent Sasanian religious policy for centuries. His efforts to centralize royal authority helped maintain the integrity of the Sasanian Empire during a period of intense internal and external challenges.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-03T09:17:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28650576286", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Bahram II (b. 250 CE) was a economist or social scientist of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of monarchs & rulers.", "newValue": "Bahram II, the fifth Sasanian King of Kings, ruled the vast Sasanian Empire from 274 to 293 CE, a critical period marked by internal religious strife, consolidating royal authority, and intermittent warfare with the Roman Empire. Born around 250 CE, his reign is primarily significant for his efforts to centralize power against a powerful nobility and the burgeoning influence of the Zoroastrian clergy, particularly under the high priest Kartir, whose rise he facilitated. His actions significantly shaped the religious and political landscape of the Sasanian state for subsequent generations.

During his nearly two-decade rule, Bahram II undertook campaigns to suppress rebellions, notably that of his brother Hormizd I Kushanshah in the eastern provinces (circa 281-283 CE), which required a multi-year effort. He also faced renewed Roman aggression under Emperor Carus in 283 CE, resulting in the temporary capture of the Sasanian capital Ctesiphon. Despite these military challenges, Bahram II dedicated considerable attention to internal religious policy, promoting the state-sponsored Zoroastrianism and persecuting Manichaeans, Christians, and Jews, a policy heavily influenced by Kartir, who achieved unprecedented ecclesiastical power during this era.

